    "q-90452": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90452",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{4},\\,1<x<3\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{13}{6},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+6X+2].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+6X+2] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+6\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[2] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+6\\text{E}[X]+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}\\,|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{13}{6}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=5$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+6X+2] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+6\\text{E}[X]+2 \\\\  & =5+6\\cdot \\frac{13}{6}+2 \\\\  & =5+13+2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
