    "q-90394": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90394",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2-2x,\\,0<x<1\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4]=3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4] & =3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+4 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
